 17592186471202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f0bb31f-6825-5c25-8cf0-baac756459f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471201} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f2aeba-3fe6-5e39-aeca-5cc3601e1920" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d277777-ae06-5b12-9091-65c43e7bf489" :question/math-academy-id "q-182241" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are finite?\n- **I.** $P({Z,t,n})$\n- **II.** $P(Z)$\n- **III.** $P({{2}^{n}∣n\\in Z})$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The power set of a finite set is finite, while the power set of an infinite set is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Set I is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\nP({Z,t,n})={∅,\\,{Z},\\,{t},\\,{n},\\,{Z,t},{Z,n},\\,{t,n},\\,{Z,t,n}}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $8.$\n\n- Set II is infinite since the underlying set $Z$ is infinite.\n\n- Set III is infinite since the underlying set ${{2}^{n}∣n\\in Z}$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4954fe5-f1fe-590a-9540-280a14606a49" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8778d1d6-56f8-53d0-84ba-767e63081689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471203} {:answer/id #uuid "2537b203-09cf-557c-81b0-c475fc88df1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid 